from author import *
CAPS=[
(41,'triage','AI failure triage: classification, retrieval and evidence','A CI failure contains an exception, test identity, build context and history. The triage system must classify product, automation, environment, test-data or unknown causes without turning a plausible narrative into a confirmed defect. Similar failures provide evidence, not proof that two incidents share a root cause.','Build a grouped-split TF-IDF baseline, retrieve approved historical failures, then optionally ask a live model for a typed explanation with evidence IDs. Map probable owners from an explicit module registry rather than asking the model to invent a person. Export a report containing category, uncertainty, similar failures and review status.','Inject an unknown failure, contradictory history and a deleted evidence ID. An accepted non-UNKNOWN decision with no supporting evidence is a release failure; a fluent explanation cannot repair that invariant. Distinguish classifier errors from retrieval misses and unsupported explanations.','Acceptance evidence includes held-out confusion metrics, retrieval Recall@K, valid citations, unknown-case escalation, p95 latency and cost per accepted task. Replay a failed case from the JSON report and show the fix without editing its expected label.'),
(42,'graphql','GraphQL test generation: schema-to-executable coverage','The generator receives a synthetic schema and query or mutation. Its job is to propose tests tied to schema and business rules, not to execute arbitrary generated code. Required arguments, nullability and enums are structural contracts; authentication, ownership and quantity limits require independent domain rules.','Parse the operation and schema with graphql-core, generate structured case definitions and validate each operation. Add explicit positive, omitted-required, invalid-enum, boundary and authorization cases. Export candidate tests with requirement IDs and execute them against the synthetic resolver before accepting the artifact.','HTTP 200 may contain GraphQL errors. A generated case with no meaningful assertion is rejected even if execution succeeds. Mutate a resolver rule to prove the accepted suite detects its target behavior; compare unique requirement coverage rather than raw candidate count.','Acceptance requires parse/validation success for intended valid cases, expected errors for invalid cases, no duplicate case IDs, complete requirement traceability and successful execution of exported candidates. Include a rejected candidate and explain the independent check that caught it.'),
(43,'rag-quality','RAG quality project: ingestion, evaluation and CI','A support assistant answers synthetic CI troubleshooting questions from approved documentation. Its quality depends on ingestion, retrieval, context packing and generation separately. A relevant answer without valid evidence is not accepted, and an unavailable index must yield a truthful fallback.','Build heading-aware chunks with document versions, retrieve with tenant filters, compare sparse and hybrid ranking, and construct cited context. Run golden cases with retrieval metrics, deterministic citation checks and an optional calibrated Ragas/DeepEval judge. Package the service and retain reports in CI.','Seed stale chunks, a cross-tenant relevant document, poisoned instructions and a no-answer query. Determine the first failing pipeline stage from candidate ranks and trace events. Test document deletion and container restart before claiming index durability.','Acceptance includes Recall@K/MRR, groundedness calibration, citation validity, zero tenant leaks, no-answer abstention, Docker/service smoke checks and a failing CI gate for a known regression. Present baseline versus candidate case-level differences with index and prompt versions.'),
(44,'automation-agent','Safe automation assistant: read, investigate and propose','The assistant investigates a failing test using approved logs and history, then proposes an explanation, locator replacement, candidate test or draft defect summary. Proposals are artifacts for review; modifying code, changing tests or filing defects are separate side effects requiring concrete authorization.','Implement a bounded state machine with typed tools, action IDs and a trace. Read failure, search history, inspect a fixture DOM and create a draft. Validate locator uniqueness and candidate test syntax. Bind approval to the exact proposed action hash with expiry before any write.','Inject repeated searches, malformed tool results, path traversal, cross-tenant IDs and an unapproved write request. The assistant must stop or escalate without broadening tools or resetting its budget. A final polite refusal does not excuse an earlier forbidden effect.','Acceptance requires goal-completion evidence, validated drafts, bounded calls/deadline, zero unauthorized effects, correct approval binding and fault recovery. Compare the safe workflow with a deterministic baseline and report unnecessary calls, p95 latency and cost.'),
(45,'platform','Final platform: typed AI services and an enforceable release gate','The final project integrates failure triage, GraphQL test candidates, RAG support and a safe automation assistant behind one authenticated service. The shared evaluation framework produces release evidence from the same synthetic data, avoiding disconnected demonstrations that cannot be compared or replayed.','Expose typed endpoints, provider adapters, retrieval, tool policy and evaluation as separate domain modules. Add Docker/Compose, bounded jobs, portable traces and JSON/HTML reports. Use a release manifest linking dataset, code, prompt, model, index, image and evaluation artifacts.','Run the entire failure matrix: provider timeout, malformed response, stale index, forbidden tool, poisoned document and load pressure. Verify visible degraded states, no secrets in reports and a nonzero release exit when any required case is missing or fails.','Acceptance requires unit/API/RAG/agent/security/performance evidence, report completeness, compatible rollback, an architecture/sequence diagram and a reviewer-ready README. Demonstrate a red gate from an injected regression and a green gate after correction using the identical versioned dataset.')]
for phase,slug,title,concept,impl,fault,evidence in CAPS:
 unit(phase,slug,title,concept+'\n\nThe portfolio result must be reproducible from the supplied synthetic fixtures and declared configuration. Keep a deterministic baseline visible alongside any live-provider results.',impl+'\n\nThe repository labs provide the domain entry points and fixture cases. Run the acceptance checks before recording project completion and keep the generated run ID in your field notes.',fault+'\n\nTrace the actual effect and retain the counterexample as a regression fixture. Fixing presentation alone does not establish that the application boundary is safe.',evidence+'\n\nPrepare a technical deep dive explaining one rejected design, one detected regression and the tradeoff behind the final implementation. Separate fixture contract evidence from live model quality claims.',
 "# Local acceptance workflow\npython -m pytest labs\npython labs/evals/runner.py --output artifacts/evaluation\n# Inspect JSON verdict and escaped HTML case report",
 rubric('Write your acceptance memo: link a reproducible run, explain a measured failure, the boundary that fixes it, and the release/rollback decision.',[{'label':'Reproducible evidence','terms':['dataset','run']},{'label':'Failure and enforcement','terms':['failure','boundary']},{'label':'Release plan','terms':['release','rollback']}],evidence),
 ('What demonstrates this project is ready for review?',['Reproducible acceptance evidence with failure cases','A polished diagram alone','A model saying the project works'],0,'Reviewable artifacts and independent checks establish the claimed behavior.'),checkpoint=False,terms=['Acceptance evidence'])
